      <article class="full-article">
        <p class="story-kicker">SCIENCE</p>
        <h1 class="article-title">Global Warming Caused by Gravity</h1>
        <p class="article-dek">Researchers at the Global Institute for Odd Theories blame planetary pull for rising temperatures, prompting traditional climatologists to pull something else.</p>
        <p class="article-meta">By Anders Kline · Friday, February 20, 2026 · GENEVA · 4 min read</p>
        
<div class="article-share" aria-label="Share this story">
  <span class="article-share-label">Share</span>
  <a href="mailto:?subject=Global%20Warming%20Caused%20by%20Gravity&amp;body=https%3A//theassociatedguess.com/article/global-warming-caused-by-gravity/">Email</a>
  <a href="https://twitter.com/intent/tweet?url=https%3A//theassociatedguess.com/article/global-warming-caused-by-gravity/&amp;text=Global%20Warming%20Caused%20by%20Gravity" rel="noopener">Post</a>
  <a href="../../reprints.html">Reprints</a>
</div>
        
        <figure class="article-hero article-hero-fit-contain">
          <img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1635070041078-e363dbe005cb?auto=format&amp;fit=crop&amp;w=800&amp;h=500&amp;q=80" alt="" />
        </figure>
        <div class="ad-slot ad-slot-in-content" data-ad-slot="inContent"></div>
        <div class="article-body">
          <p>GENEVA  -  Scientists at the Global Institute for Odd Theories published a paper Tuesday arguing that global warming stems not from greenhouse gases but from gravity slowly compressing the atmosphere against Earth&#x27;s surface &quot;like a very large, very patient hand.&quot; Director Prof. Viktor Hale said rotational momentum pulls air molecules downward, increasing friction and, therefore, heat. &quot;Everyone talks about carbon,&quot; Hale told reporters. &quot;Nobody respects the downward force that keeps us all from achieving our orbital dreams.&quot;</p>
<p>The institute&#x27;s model predicts hotter summers, moodier oceans, and a measurable increase in the sensation that Mondays are heavier than other days. According to the team’s calculations, as gravity exerts more pressure on the atmosphere, it traps heat like an overzealous baker sealing a pie crust with extra dough. &quot;It&#x27;s not just about carbon dioxide,&quot; Hale explained. &quot;It&#x27;s about the fundamental forces of nature working against us.&quot;</p>
<p>Mainstream climatologists dismissed the theory during an emergency webinar titled &quot;Please Read the Existing Literature.&quot; Dr. Amara Singh called the paper &quot;creative&quot; and &quot;wrong in units we did not know existed.&quot; She continued, &quot;The Earth is a complex system with many factors contributing to climate change. We&#x27;ve seen this before - every time someone suggests something as absurd as gravity causing global warming.&quot;</p>
<p>Hale countered that peer review is &quot;gravity for ideas&quot; and insisted his team welcomes scrutiny after they finish building a desktop planet. He added, &quot;We&#x27;re not saying we can build a planet in our lab anytime soon, but the process of trying will be enlightening.&quot; The institute has already secured funding from an anonymous benefactor who believes that if gravity can be harnessed for energy, it could revolutionize the world.</p>
<p>Policy implications remain unclear. One aide to a European energy minister asked whether anti-gravity research could qualify for green subsidies. Hale said no, but offered branded weighted blankets as a consolation prize. &quot;We&#x27;re not in the business of making money,&quot; he stated. &quot;But if you want something to help you sleep better at night, we&#x27;ve got you covered.&quot;</p>
<p>Environmental groups urged the public to continue reducing emissions rather than waiting for Earth to lose mass. Hale agreed emissions matter, then whispered, &quot;Gravity matters louder.&quot; He added, &quot;We&#x27;re not saying our theory is the only one out there, but it&#x27;s certainly worth considering in the grand scheme of things.&quot;</p>
<p>The United Nations climate panel declined to add the theory to its summary but noted &quot;increased public interest in physics among people who dislike graphs.&quot; Panel member Dr. Elena Novak commented, &quot;It&#x27;s always interesting when someone tries to bring a new perspective to an old problem. But let&#x27;s not forget that the science of climate change is well-established and peer-reviewed.&quot;</p>
<p>Hale&#x27;s team next plans to investigate whether tides are caused by the moon or by the ocean &quot;remembering something sad.&quot; He explained, &quot;We think the ocean might have some kind of emotional memory - like a person who can&#x27;t let go of a bad breakup. It&#x27;s a bit of a stretch, but we&#x27;re scientists, and we like to explore all possibilities.&quot;</p>
<p>In Geneva, local residents expressed mixed feelings about the theory. One resident, Maria Martinez, said, &quot;I don&#x27;t know if I should be worried or just laugh it off. But gravity is definitely something that keeps me grounded every day.&quot; Another, John Smith, added, &quot;It&#x27;s funny how many things we take for granted until someone comes along and questions them.&quot;</p>
<p>The Global Institute for Odd Theories has already attracted a small but dedicated following on social media. Their Twitter handle @GravityWarming has over 500 followers who eagerly await updates on their desktop planet project. One fan, Sarah Johnson, commented, &quot;I love the idea that gravity is keeping us all warm. It makes me feel cozy.&quot;</p>
<p>As the debate continues, Prof. Viktor Hale remains unfazed by the criticism. He said, &quot;We&#x27;re just doing our job as scientists - exploring every possible explanation for global warming. And who knows? Maybe one day we&#x27;ll have a planet in our lab and be able to prove that gravity is indeed the culprit.&quot;</p>
<p>In the meantime, the world will continue to grapple with the complexities of climate change, hoping that somewhere out there, someone might just find the key to understanding it all - whether through carbon emissions or the gravitational pull of an overworked atmosphere.</p>
          
        </div>
        
